Today I learned about computational thinking and its four key components: decomposition, abstraction, pattern recognition, and algorithm thinking. Before this lesson, I usually just jumped into solving problems without any clear strategy. Now I realize that breaking a big problem into smaller pieces makes it much easier to handle.

I also learned that computer science is not just about coding, but about problem solving, processing information, and understanding the effects of computation in our daily lives. This broader definition helps me see CS as a useful tool for many areas, not just technology.

By applying computational thinking to my study schedule problem, I found that the process was very logical and effective. Decomposition helped me see all the parts clearly, pattern recognition made me aware of my habits, and algorithm thinking gave me a practical action plan. I feel more confident about managing my time now.
